<!-- livebook:{"persist_outputs":true} -->

# Example

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:associations, github: "Wigny/associations"},
  {:req, "~> 0.7"}
])
```

## The data source

[JSONPlaceholder](https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com) serves users, the posts they wrote and the comments those posts received, over three endpoints that filter by query parameter. Each endpoint answers a whole set of values at once, `/comments?postId=1&postId=2` returning the comments of both posts, which is what lets every search below be a single request.

The client turns a schema, the fields being searched and the values being searched for into one request. Every schema says which path it lives at, which query parameter each of its fields is called, and how to read a record out of the response.

```elixir
defmodule Client do
  def list(schema, fields, values) do
    params =
      for row <- values, {field, value} <- Enum.zip(fields, row) do
        {schema.param(field), value}
      end

    %{body: body} =
      Req.get!(
        base_url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/",
        url: schema.path(),
        params: params
      )

    Enum.map(body, &schema.cast/1)
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Client, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 10, ...>>, ...}
```

## The schemas

Plain structs, with no knowledge of one another.

```elixir
defmodule User do
  defstruct [:id, :name]

  def path, do: "/users"

  def param(:id), do: "id"

  def cast(params) do
    struct!(__MODULE__, id: Map.fetch!(params, "id"), name: Map.fetch!(params, "name"))
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, User, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 12, ...>>, ...}
```

```elixir
defmodule Post do
  defstruct [:id, :user_id, :title, :body]

  def path, do: "/posts"

  def param(:id), do: "id"
  def param(:user_id), do: "userId"

  def cast(params) do
    struct!(__MODULE__,
      id: Map.fetch!(params, "id"),
      user_id: Map.fetch!(params, "userId"),
      title: Map.fetch!(params, "title"),
      body: Map.fetch!(params, "body")
    )
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Post, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 14, ...>>, ...}
```

```elixir
defmodule Comment do
  defstruct [:id, :post_id, :name, :body]

  def path, do: "/comments"

  def param(:id), do: "id"
  def param(:post_id), do: "postId"

  def cast(params) do
    struct!(__MODULE__,
      id: Map.fetch!(params, "id"),
      post_id: Map.fetch!(params, "postId"),
      name: Map.fetch!(params, "name"),
      body: Map.fetch!(params, "body")
    )
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Comment, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 14, ...>>, ...}
```

## The declarations

`Blog` is the only module that knows how the three relate. Its `list/3` leaves the fields open, so the one clause answers every schema, and it logs every call it is asked for so that the requests each load makes are visible below.

```elixir
defmodule Blog do
  use Associations

  require Logger

  @impl true
  def list(schema, fields, values) do
    Logger.info("list #{inspect(schema)} by #{inspect(fields)} for #{length(values)} rows")

    Client.list(schema, fields, values)
  end

  association User do
    has_many :posts, Post
  end

  association Post do
    belongs_to :author, User, foreign_key: :user_id
    has_many :comments, Comment
  end

  association Comment do
    belongs_to :post, Post
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:module, Blog, <<70, 79, 82, 49, 0, 0, 25, ...>>, ...}
```

## Loading one record

`load/2` searches for the association of a single record. A `has_many` gives back a list.

```elixir
user = %User{id: 1}

posts = Blog.load(user, :posts)
```

